title: Fundamentalism versus Vain Philosophy
description: Where the Scripture presents mystery, the fundamentalist stops. A lesson on answering error with the text of the Bible and not with the vain philosophy of men, traced through the early church controversies.
output: lessons/heresies/fundamentalism-vs-vain-philosophy.html

# Converted from the hand-made page lessons/heresies/fundamentalism-vs-vain-philosophy.html (26 slides, same order).
# The "Hegelian Cycle" slide, which had nine tap-to-open steps, is one slide with nine timeline steps.
# The teacher's lesson plan (fundamentalism-vs-vain-philosophy-lesson-plan.html) is a separate page and is not changed.

=== Title
--- Fundamentalism versus Vain Philosophy [title]
overline: A lesson on the Bible alone
heading: Fundamen­talism versus Vain Philosophy
sub: Where the Scripture presents mystery, the fundamentalist stops.

=== I. The Mystery of Godliness
--- The Mystery of Godliness
kicker: I
scripture: 1 Timothy 3:16 | mark: great is the mystery of godliness
lead: Where the text of scripture presents mystery, “great is the mystery of godliness,” the fundamentalist stops, where the philosopher and the theologian continue, pressing on in their vain imagination.

--- Revelation and Faith
lead: The truth is not found by the reasoning of men. It is by the revelation of scripture, and by understanding that some things our finite brain cannot comprehend. And accepting those things by faith.
callout: God revealed it. God did not explain it. We believe it.
note: Compare scripture with scripture
refs: Deuteronomy 29:29; 1 Corinthians 13:12

=== II. Heresy Answered with Heresy
--- Heresy Answered with Heresy
kicker: II
lead: We see people debate the various vain philosophies of men used to explain the position of the Father, the godhood of the incarnate Jesus, the Trinity, the person of the Spirit, and the unity of it all, in the fallible and weak terms of men.
callout: Biblical fundamentalism is superior to theology. Heresy answered with heresy is not how we find the truth.
note: Compare scripture with scripture
refs: 1 Corinthians 1:20; 2 Timothy 2:23

--- The Trap of the “Closer” Answer
lead: Way too many Christians, saved, Bible-believing Christians at that, see an answer to a clear heresy that is closer to the truth, and accept that doctrine as solid. They lean on the understanding of traditions. The consensus of the crowd.
p: Closer to the truth is not the truth. An error that corrects another error is still an error, and the man who adopts it has only traded one tradition of men for another.
note: Compare scripture with scripture
refs: Proverbs 3:5-6

=== III. The Back-and-Forth Away from the Bible
--- The Back-and-Forth Away from the Bible
kicker: III
image: lessons/heresies/vain-philosophy-plate.jpg | Two statues, one marble and one gold, pull on a tangle of chains on either side of a column of light, under the words of 1 Timothy 3:16. Thesis (action) is listed on the marble side and antithesis (reaction) on the gold side. | The pure light of preserved Scripture, untouched by either side.
lead: Each error provoked a reaction. Each reaction became its own error. Thesis produced antithesis, and both sides pulled on the same chains: the categories of Greek and Latin philosophy. Neither one stood in the light of the plain text.

--- The Hegelian Cycle, c. 90–381 AD
note: Thesis (action) · Antithesis (reaction) · Synthesis
step: c. 90–150 AD | **1. The Gnostic Spark** (underlying thesis). *Valentinus, Marcion, and the Gnostics.* Introduced Greek dualism: matter is evil, spirit is good. Denied the real physical body of Jesus (Docetism), or claimed the divine Christ-spirit only temporarily inhabited the man Jesus. **Abandoned:** “the Word was made flesh” [[John 1:14]] and the reality of Christ’s true humanity [[1 John 4:2-3]].
step: c. 150–165 AD | **2. The Apologists’ Logos Theory** (first antithesis). *Justin Martyr and the early Greek Apologists.* Answered the objections of pagans and Jews with Greek Logos philosophy instead of the plain text. Presented the Father as the supreme, hidden God and the Son as a secondary, generated instrument, “another God” made reasonable to the Greek mind. The Gnostics had already brought Greek dualism into the church through the back door. Now the defenders of the faith brought Greek philosophy in through the front door. Heretic and apologist were working in the same categories. **Result:** Birthed subordinationism, the Son inherently lesser than the Father, and set the terms for the back-and-forth that followed.
step: c. 190–268 AD | **3. Dynamic Monarchianism / Adoptionism** (reactionary thesis). *Theodotus of Byzantium, Paul of Samosata.* Reacted against the “two-God” trajectory of the Apologists. Sought to protect strict monotheism by human logic. **Result:** Taught that Jesus was a mere man adopted by God at His baptism, denying His eternal deity.
step: c. 200–250 AD | **4. Modalistic Monarchianism / Sabellianism** (counter-antithesis). *Noetus, Praxeas, Sabellius.* Over-corrected against Adoptionism to defend Christ’s absolute deity. **Result:** Taught that the Father, Son, and Holy Ghost are only temporary modes or masks of one divine person (Modalism, Patripassianism).
step: c. 210 AD | **5. Tertullian’s Materialistic Legalism** (philosophical synthesis). *Tertullian of Carthage.* Sought to defeat Modalism without returning to Adoptionism. Imported Latin legal and physical terminology: *una substantia, tres personae*, “one substance, three persons.” **Result:** Defined the Father as the whole substance and the Son as a portion derived from it.
step: c. 230–250 AD | **6. Origen’s Platonic Invention** (Alexandrian thesis). *Origen of Alexandria.* Blended earlier ideas with Neo-Platonic philosophy. Invented “Eternal Generation”: the Son is timelessly and continuously begotten from the Father’s substance. **Result:** Forced the Bible word “begotten” out of its scriptural context.
step: c. 318–325 AD | **7. The Arian Outbreak** (logical conclusion, ultimate thesis). *Arius of Alexandria.* Took subordinationism and eternal generation to their logical extreme. Declared “there was a time when the Son was not.” **Abandoned:** The eternal deity of the Word, reducing Christ to a created, exalted being [[John 1:1-3]].
step: 325 AD | **8. The Nicene Creed and the State Synthesis** (imperial antithesis). *Athanasius, Constantine, the Council of Nicaea.* Answered Arianism with more philosophy rather than pure Scripture. Canonized the extra-biblical term *homoousios*, “of the same substance.” **Result:** Locked Origen’s “eternally begotten” language into a state-enforced creed.
step: 325–381 AD | **9. Post-Nicene Fragmentation** (the cycle continues). The new philosophical vocabulary produced further warring camps: Semi-Arians, Homoiousians, Anomoeans, and more. **Result:** Human creeds generated more human error instead of biblical unity.

--- The Core Pattern
callout: Each step refused to stop where Scripture stops.
lead: Thesis produced antithesis. Both remained inside Greek or Latin philosophical categories. The result was progressive distance from the plain statements of the preserved text, and from the humble acceptance of mystery.
table: The philosopher’s way | The fundamentalist’s way
tr: Answer error with a counter-theory. | Answer error with the text.
tr: Borrow the world’s vocabulary to close the gap. | Speak in the words the Holy Ghost teacheth. [[1 Corinthians 2:13]]
tr: Enforce the result by creed and council. | Stop where Scripture stops, and believe.

=== IV. Where Scripture Stops, We Stop
--- Where Scripture Stops, We Stop
kicker: IV
lead: The fundamentalist does not feel obligated to supply the missing metaphysical machinery that finite minds invent to close the gap. And we don’t need to take sides in the back-and-forth debates of differing heretical schools of thought.
p: We are not on the stone side or the gold side. We are not obligated to pick the lesser error. We stand where the light falls: on what God said.

--- No Prepackaged Beliefs
lead: Fundamentalists should never accept presuppositions and systematics that require us to adopt a set of beliefs that come prepackaged, regurgitating apologetics because others before us accepted them whole cloth.
p: **Always search the scripture to see if it is so.** And if you cannot draw a clear, direct understanding of a doctrine or view from plain language and context, then it is okay, and in fact wise, to leave it to faith and admit your ignorance as fallible, finite man.
note: Compare scripture with scripture
refs: Acts 17:11; Deuteronomy 29:29

=== V. Pure Bible, Not “Sola Scriptura”
--- Pure Bible, Not “Sola Scriptura”
kicker: V
lead: The fundamentalist rejects the theologian’s “Sola Scriptura” for pure Bible. The theologians use the logic of men, the writings of church fathers and reformers and past generations of men, to claim the Bible should be the final arbiter of doctrine.
p: That sliver of daylight given to the authority of tradition and man’s philosophy invites error just as much as the other attempts to answer bad doctrine.
panel-warn: “Final arbiter” | The Bible judges, but tradition, the fathers, and the reformers sit at the table.
panel: Only authority | The Bible alone speaks for matters of faith and practice. Nothing else sits at the table.

--- Scripture Compared with Scripture
lead: Taking scripture compared to scripture as the only means of finding truth, and allowing the Bible to be the only authority for matters of faith and practice, avoids these traps.
scripture: 2 Timothy 3:16-17 | mark: throughly furnished unto all good works
take: Throughly furnished. The man of God does not need a supplement.

--- Liberty on Secondary Matters
lead: The general understanding of Romans 14, that on secondary matters men may disagree and be fully persuaded in their own minds, without judging another man’s servant, makes fundamentalism the way.
callout: Primary doctrine is clear. God is not the author of confusion. But where we see through a glass darkly, there is room for brotherly disagreement.
note: Compare scripture with scripture
refs: Romans 14:4-5; 1 Corinthians 14:33; 1 Corinthians 13:12

=== VI. The Witness of God Is Greater
--- Three That Bear Record
kicker: VI
scripture: 1 John 5:7 | mark: these three are one
lead: The Bible does not demand that we figure out the metaphysical mechanics of how three can be one. It simply states the fact of their heavenly record and their absolute unity, leaving no room for human rationalization.

--- What Scripture Does Not Say
lead: Scripture doesn’t get into the mode, the essence, the hypostatic union, the filioque, or any other nonsense used to debate endlessly the vain wisdom of man.
panel-warn: The words of man’s wisdom | Mode. Essence. Substance. Hypostatic union. Filioque. Eternal generation.
panel: The words the Holy Ghost teacheth | The revealed names, Father, Word, and Holy Ghost, and the fact that these three are one. [[1 John 5:7]]
p: It identifies them by their revealed biblical names, Father, Word, and Holy Ghost, and states the undeniable fact that these three are one.

--- By Water and Blood
scripture: 1 John 5:6 | mark: by water and blood
lead: God anchors the reality of Jesus Christ to historical facts. He was born of water, the incarnation, and He came by blood, the death and resurrection. And the person of the Spirit bears witness to Christ’s factual existence and the truth of the gospel.
callout: There is no need to explain what we know is true. It is just true.

--- The Witness of God Is Greater
scripture: 1 John 5:9 | mark: the witness of God is greater
lead: The theologian and the seminarian and the other “experts” seek to gain our confidence in them. In their arguments and their theories and their jargon.
callout: But the witness of God is greater.

--- The Witness in Himself
scripture: 1 John 5:10 | mark: hath the witness in himself
lead: We have the Spirit of truth, who teaches us all things. Who does not speak of Himself, but the good things of God.
note: Compare scripture with scripture
refs: John 14:26; John 16:13

--- Comparing Spiritual Things with Spiritual
scripture: 1 Corinthians 2:12-13 | mark: comparing spiritual things with spiritual
callout: What more do we need?

--- This Is the True God
scripture: 1 John 5:20 | mark: This is the true God, and eternal life
lead: This speaks expressly of the truth we have known, and the gift of eternal life, that we may know Him. The truth that is the true God.
p: So don’t let theologians and men wise in their own conceits tell you that you need to be “educated” as they are to know the truth.
note: Compare scripture with scripture
refs: Romans 12:16

=== VII. Beware Lest Any Man Spoil You
--- Lest Any Man Beguile You
kicker: VII
scripture: Colossians 2:1-4 | mark: lest any man should beguile you with enticing words
lead: The beguiling men with enticing words seek to lead us from the truth. Paul in the Spirit is giving prophecy that applies to Christians today as much as it did the Colossians and the Laodiceans.
p: Our hearts may be comforted with the full assurance of understanding, even as we acknowledge the mystery of God. Of the Father, and of Christ, in whom are hid **all** the treasures of wisdom and knowledge.

--- Rooted and Built Up in Him
scripture: Colossians 2:6-9 | mark: Beware lest any man spoil you through philosophy and vain deceit
lead: We have received Christ, sealed by the Spirit; we are rooted in Him. We are called to walk in Him. And because we are established in the faith, secure in our knowledge of the truth, we can never deny Him. But we can be spoiled by deceivers.
p: Led away by vain traditions of men, following the ways of the world, the logic of men. Following the theology and theory and disputations of denominations and schools of thought, instead of Christ.

--- All the Fulness of the Godhead Bodily
lead: The fulness of the Godhead is stated as a fact, in Christ. The scripture does not seek to define His “essence,” substance, or the mechanics of that fulness. God’s word simply declares it as truth, and then warns against the very methods that later generations of men would use to elaborate it.
panel-warn: What Paul warned against | Philosophy. Vain deceit. The tradition of men. The rudiments, the elementary principles, of the world: the natural man’s view.
panel-warn: What came after | Vain and puffed-up philosophy. Groups of men with creeds and confessions that add to and take away from the things of Christ.
note: Teaching for doctrines the commandments of men
refs: Matthew 15:9

--- Intruding into Things Not Seen
scripture: Colossians 2:16-18 | mark: intruding into those things which he hath not seen
lead: These are the tip of the iceberg: the various ways false teachers use their own imagination to invent how we should live and what we should do, condemning those of us who place our trust entirely in Christ and His word.

=== Closing
--- The Witness Is God’s
lead: You do not need the seminary’s vocabulary, the council’s creed, or the philosopher’s system to know the Son of God. The Father, the Word, and the Holy Ghost bear record, and these three are one. Christ came by water and blood. In Him dwelleth all the fulness of the Godhead bodily. Where God has spoken, we believe. Where God has drawn the curtain of mystery, we stop, and we worship.
take: God Himself testifies with us the truth of His Son. Amen.

--- Memory Verse
kicker: For Discussion
big: Colossians 2:8 | mark: Beware lest any man spoil you through philosophy and vain deceit
- [[1 Timothy 3:16]] calls the mystery of godliness “without controversy.” Why did men turn it into centuries of controversy?
- Look at the cycle. At what point should each side have simply stopped and said, “Thus saith the Lord, and no further”?
- What is the difference between the Bible as the “final arbiter” and the Bible as the only authority? Why does the sliver of daylight matter?
- Name a doctrine you were taught that you have never checked against the plain text. How would you search the scripture “whether those things were so”?
- Where is the line between primary doctrine, which is clear, and the secondary matters of Romans 14? How do we keep brotherly love on the second without compromising the first?
